"What is the role of ARTEMIS in DNA repair during V(D)J recombination, and why is this important for immune system function?","<bos><start_of_turn>user
What is the role of ARTEMIS in DNA repair during V(D)J recombination, and why is this important for immune system function?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","ARTEMAS is a critical enzyme in DNA repair during V(D)J recombination. ARTEMAS is a DNA exonuclease that plays a critical role in the repair of double-strand breaks (DSBs) that occur during V(DJ) recombination. This process is essential for generating diverse B and T cell receptors, which are essential for mounting an effective immune response.  

The process of V(D)J recombination is essential for generating the diversity of B and T cell receptors, enabling the immune system to recognize and respond to a wide range of foreign antigens. This process involves the random joining of variable (V), D (in the delta chain of the Ig heavy chain), and joining (J) regions of immunoglobulin genes (B cells) or T-cell receptor genes (T cells). The process is not error-free, and generates DSBs in the DNA. ARTEMAS is involved in repairing these breaks, ensuring the integrity of the rearranged genes.  

