","The coordination geometry of the iron (III) ion in iron (III) tetraphenylporphyrin complex is **distiortically octahedral**, which is often referred to as a **""μ-trigonal-bidentate""** geometry. This is because the central iron ion is coordinated to four oxygen atoms of the tetraphenylene porphyrin macrocycle, and two additional donor groups (e.g., chloride⁻ or water) are coordinated to the iron ion, leading to six coordination sites. However, the geometry is not perfectly octahedral due to steric repulsion and electronic effects.  

The geometry is distorted because the four oxygen atoms of the porphyrin macrocycle are not equidistant from the iron ion, leading to a distorted octahedral structure.  

The μ-trigetal-bidentate structure arises because the two additional donor groups (e.g., Cl⁻ or H₂O) bridge the two iron centers in a dimeric complex.  

Thus, the geometry is best described as a distorted octahedral with a μ-trigonal-bidentate arrangement.  

