</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The entropy change (ΔS) for a reaction is calculated using the formula:
\[
\Delta S = \sum \ln(n_{\text{products}}) - \sum \ln(n_{\text{reactants})}
\]
where \( n \) is the number of moles of each substance.

For the given reaction:
\[
2 \text{PbS(s)} + 3 \text{O}_2(g] \rightarrow 2 \text{PbO(s)} + 2 \text{SO}_2(g)
\]
The molar entropities are:
\[
\Delta S = \Delta S_{\text{products}} - \Delta S_{\text{reactants}} = \sum \Delta S_{\text{products}} - \sum \Delta S_{\text{reactants}}
\]
Substituting the given values:
\[
\Delta S = (2 \times 66.5) + (2 \times 248.2) - (2 \times 91.2) - (3 \times 205.1)
\]
\[
\Delta S = 133 + 502 - 192 - 610 = 635 - 802 = -167 \, \text{J/mol}
\]

Thus, the entropy change is \(\boxed{-167 \, \text{J/mol K}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

