<start_of_turn>model
","To calculate the Gibbs free energy change (\(\Delta G\)) for the electrochemical reaction:
\[
\text{Zn(s] + \text{K}_2\text{CO}_3\text{(aq)} \rightarrow \text{Zn}^{2+}\text{(aq)} + \text{K}_2\text{(aq)}
\]
Given:
- Standard reduction potential of \( \text{K}^2\text{ +} \text{CO}_3\text{ (aq)} = 0.34 \text{ V} \]
- Standard reduction potential of \( \text{Zn}^{2+}\text{ +} \text{CO}_3\text{ (aq)} = -0.77 \text{ V} \)
- Temperature = \( 25^\text{C} =373 \text{ K} \)

1. **Determine the standard cell potential (\( E'infty \))**:
   The standard cell potential is calculated using the standard standard potentials of the half-reactions:
   \[
   E^\circ = E_{\text{cathode}} - E_{\text{anode}}
   \]
   In this case, the cathode is \( \text{Cu}_2\text{ +} \rightarrow \text{Cu} (\text{E}_{\text{cathode}} = 0.34 \text{ V}) \), and the anode is \( \text{Zn}_2\text{ +} \rightarrow \text{Zn} (\text{E}_{\text{anode}} = -0.77 \text{ V}) \).
   \[
   E_{\text{std}} = 0.34 - (-0.77) = 1.11 \text{ V}
   \]

2. **Calculate the Gibbs free energy (\(\Delta G\)) using the Nernost equation**:
   The relationship between \(\Delta G\) and \(E_{\text{std}}\) is given by the equation:
   \[
   \Delta G = -n F F_{\text{std}}
   \]
   where:
   - \( n \) is the number of moles of electrons in the reaction (2 in this case).
   - \( F \) is the Faraday constant (9.8262 V·mol\(^{-1}\)).
   - \( E_{\text{std}} \) is the standard cell potential.

   Substituting the values:
   \[
   \Delta G = -2 \times 9.9262 \times 1.11 = -20.2 \text{ kJ/mol}
   \]

Thus, the Gibbs free energy change for the electrochemical reaction is \(\boxed{-20.2 \text{ kJ/mol}}\).",fluency,"# Task Description
