","The pH of the solution has a significant effect on the electrokinetic potential of colloidal particles. This effect is due to the ionization of the charged surface of the particles and the resulting changes in the surface potential. The ionic strength of the solution also plays a role by influencing the thickness of the electrical double layer, which in turn affects the electrokinetic potential.

1. The electrokinetic potential (EP), also known as the zeta potential (ζ), is the potential at the slipping layer of a particle in an electric field. It is a measure of the surface charge of the particles and the surrounding solution.

2. The zeta potential is determined by the surface charge of the particles, which can be positive or negative, and the thickness of the electrical double layer. The electrical double layer is formed by the surface charge and the counter-ion layer that forms around the surface to neutralize the charge.

3. The pH of the solution affects the surface charge of the particles by controlling the ionization of functional groups on the particle surface. For example, in the case of carboxylated particles, the surface charge is negative at high pH (due to deprotonation of the carboxyl groups) and neutral at low pH (due to protonation).

4. The ionic strength of the solution affects the thickness of the electrical double layer. At high ionic strength, the counter-ions are more mobile and can penetrate into the double layer, reducing its thickness. This effect is known as the Debye effect.

