For discrete random variables, we have
\[ \text{E}( X^2) = \sum P( X = x) x^2 = 0 \]
For continuous random variables, we have
\[ \text{e}( X^2) = \int P( X = y) y^2 \, d y = 0 \]

If \( X \) is a discrete random variable, then \( \sum P( X = x) x^2 = 0 \).
Since \( x^2 \geq 0 \) for all \( x \), we have that \( P( X = x) x^2 = 0 \) for all \( x \).
Since \( x^2 \geq 0 \), this means that \( P( X = x) = 0 \) for all \( x \neq 0 \).
Thus, the only possible value is \( X = 0 \), and \( P( X = 0) = 1 \).

If \( X \) is a continuous random variable, then \( \int P( X = y) y^2 \, d y = 0 \).
