For example, in the case of a fair coin toss, the event space is the set of possible outcomes, which is $S = \text{Heads, Tails}$. The probability measure assigns the probability 1/2 to each event, so $\mathbb{P}(\text{Heads}) = 1/2$ and $\mathbb{P}(\text{Tails}) = 1/2$. 

