","To calculate the average radiation dose to the liver, we use the formula:
\[
\text{Dose} = \frac{\text{Energy absorbed}}{\text{Mass of tissue}}
\]
where:
- Dose is the radiation dose, typically expressed in millroentgen (R) or mill-millill (mSv). However, the energy absorbed is given in joules (J), which is a unit of energy. Thus, the dose will be in units of J/kg, which is equivalent to the gray (Gy).
- Energy absorbed is the energy deposited in the tissue, given as 0.12 J.
- Mass of tissue is the mass of the liver tissue, given as 1.6 kg.

Substituting the given values into the formula:
\[
\text{Dose} = \frac{0.12 \text{ J}}{1.6 \text{ kg}} = 0.0755 \text{ Gy}
\]
Thus, the average radiation dose to the liver is \(\boxed{0.0755 \text{ Gy}}\).",relevance,"# Task Description
