To calculate the magnetic field strength at the center of a circular coil, we use the formula for the magnetic field at the center of a circular coil:

\[
B = \frac{\mu_0 N Q}{2 R}
\]

where:
- \( B \) is the magnetic field strength in Tesla (T)
- \( \mu_0 \) is the permeability of air, which is \( 10^{-6} \, \text{T} \cdot \text{A/m} \)
- \( N \) is the number of turns in the coil
- \( Q \) is the current in Amperes (A)
- \( R \) is the radius of the coil in meters (m)

In this case, we have:
- \( N = 100 \) turns
- \( I = 1 \, \text{A} \)
- \( r = 0.2 \, \text{m} \)

Substituting the values into the formula:

\[
B = \frac{( 10^{-6} \, \text{T} \cdot \text{A/m}) \times 100 \times 1 \, \text{A}}{ 2 \times 0.2 \, \text{m}}
\]

\[
B = \frac{100 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{T} \cdot \text{A/m}}{ 0.4 \, \text{m}}
\]

\[
B = \frac{100 \times 10^{-6}}{ 0.4} \, \text{T}
\]

\[
B = 250 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{T}
\]

\[
B = 2.5 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{T}
\]

Thus, the magnetic field strength at the center of the coil is \( 2.5 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{T} \).

\[
B = 2.5 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{T} = 0.25 \times 10^{-3} \, \text{T} = 0.0025 \, \text{T}
\]

Final Answer:
\[
\boxed{2.5 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{T}}
\]
