A polycrystalline aluminium alloy contains hard particles with a diameter of \(10^{-8}\) m and an average center-to-center spacing of \(6 \times 10^{-8}\) m in the slip planes. The shear modulus \(G\) of aluminium is 26 GN m\(^{-2}\), and the Burgers vector \(b\) is 0.286 nm. Estimate the contribution of these particles to the tensile yield strength \(\sigma_{yr}\) of the alloy.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The tensile yield strength of the alloy is estimated using the stress tensor model, where the stress is distributed between the slip planes and the hard particles. The Burgers stress is the stress required to break a dislocation at the tip of a crystal plane. The stress tensor model assumes that the stress is distributed between the slip planes and the hard particles, and the tensile yield strength is the sum of the stress at the slip plane and the stress at the particles. The Burgers stress is given by:

\[
\sigma_{br} = \frac{G b}{2 d}
\]

where:
- \(G\) is the shear modulus of the alloy (26 GN m\(^{-2}\))
- \(b\) is the Burgers vector (0.286 nm)
- \(d\) is the distance between the hard particles (6 × 10\(^{-8}\) m)

Substituting the given values:
\[
\sigma_{br} = \frac{26 \times 10^3 \text{ N/m}^2 \times 10^{-10} \text{ m}}{2 \times 6 \times 10^{-8} \text{ m}} = \frac{26 \times 10^{-7}}{12 \times 10^{-8}} = 2.08 \text{ N/m}^2 = 2.08 \text{ MPa}
\]

The number of particles per unit unit area is:
\[
n = \frac{1}{d^2} = \frac{1}{(6 \times 10^{-8} \text{ m})^2} = \frac{1}{36 \times 10^{-16}} \approx 27..1 \text{ m}^{-2}
\]

The tensile yield strength \(\sigma_{yr}\) is given by:
\[
