","To calculate the change in Gibbs free energy for the reaction, we use the standard Gibbs free energy change for the reaction:  
\[
\Delta G = \sum n_m c_m \Delta G_0(s]
\]  
where \( n_m \) is the stoichiometric coefficient of the species and \( \Delta G_0(s] \) is the standard Gibbs free energy of formation of the substance.  

The reaction is:  
\[
2 \text{H}_2 + \text{O}_2 \rightarrow 2 \text{H}_2\text{O}
\]  

The standard Gibbs free energy of formation (\(\Delta G_0\)) for the reactants and products is:  
- \(\text{H}_2\): \(\Delta G_0 = 0\) (since it is a gas and in its standard state)  
- \(\text{O}_2\): \(\Delta G_0 = 0\) (since it is a gas and in its standard state)  
- \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\): \(\Delta G_0 = -237.1 \, \text{kJ/mol}\) (given)  

The change in Gibbs free energy for the reaction is:  
\[
\Delta G = 2 \times 0 + 1 \times 0 + 2 \times (-237.1 \, \text{kJ/mol}) = - 474.2 \, \text{kJ}
\]  

Since the reaction is at a constant temperature and pressure, the change in Gibbs free energy is:  
\[
\Delta G = \Delta G_0 + RT \ln \frac{\Delta \Delta P}{\Pi P_0}
\]  
However, the problem does not provide any information about the partial pressures of the reactants or products, so we can use the standard Gibbs free energy change.  

Thus, the change in Gibbs free energy for the reaction is \(\boxed{-474.2 \, \text{kJ}}\).",fluency,"# Task Description
